I think I have found a fix for this, see also: https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/issues/74#issuecomment-405345724

On 23 July 2018 at 19:14, Vahan Aslanyan <vaha...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Hi Vincent, I used desiredtz to specify my timezone, my timestamp looked something like 2016-11-16T18:53:00-0800. My coworker is working with the same data and running the same commands without getting errors, so I assumed it was a fault of my own encoding. I can share my modified code if needed. Thank you!
>
> On Sunday, July 22, 2018 at 6:44:54 AM UTC-7, Vincent van Hees wrote:
> > Hi Vahan,
> > Can you clarify whether (1) you used argument desiredtz to specify the timezone you are in, and (2) what timestamp format you have?
> > If what you describe is a bug in GGIR then it would be good to document it as an issue <https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/issues>, such that someone can try to develop a generic solution.
> > If everyone creates their own local solution then we would all be doing a lot of double work. Sharing experiences and documenting issues is an important way to contribute to open source software.
> > Thanks, Vincent
> >
> >
> > On 17 July 2018 at 19:17, Vahan Aslanyan <vaha...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > So my problem was coming from how the time and date were encoded. Our data comes from Los Angeles, so we needed time in PST. Look at the source code for part 5  and run it line by line you will notice when the error occurs. I had to write a gsub() around my date\time to make it compatible with the character input of date and time ( getting rid of timezone and T in between date and time). I am not posting the source code since this is a local solution and might not work for general public and I don't want to confuse people) but I changed the code for time on line 133 to  time = gsub("-0800","",gsub("T"," ",IMP$metashort[,1])).
> > >
> > > Then I saved the file as g.part5.R in my local working directory and ran the following in my script before the g.shell.GGIR():
> > >
> > > source("~/g.part5.R")
> > > > assignInNamespace("g.part5",g.part5,ns="GGIR")
> > > > source('~/getfolderstructure.R')
> > > > source('~/identify_levels.R')
> > >
> > > Hope this helps (download the source code from github)
> > >
